{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-277826",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-1}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-1}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-1}}+2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y-1} & =\\frac{3}{x-2} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{2y-1})^{3} & ={(\\frac{3}{x-2})}^{3} \\\\ 2y-1 & =\\frac{27}{(x-2)^{3}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{27}{(x-2)^{3}}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{27}{2(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{27}{2(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{1}{2},\\,f(x)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
